    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Heir to the army and the Greek alliance built by his father Philip II, Alexander became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> at 20 and in ten years of campaigning from 334 BCE overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Persian Empire</a>.</li><li>The conquests were made with massacres and enslavement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, Gaza and Massaga, and his adoption of Persian customs and the killing of his own officers cost him the sympathy of many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a>.</li><li>He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> at 32 without an obvious heir; his generals fought for forty years over an empire that broke into Hellenistic kingdoms, through which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> and culture spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Alexander's body was laid in a gold sarcophagus filled with honey. His companion Ptolemy seized the funeral cortege on its way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and took the body to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where a later king, Ptolemy IX Lathyros, replaced the gold sarcophagus with a glass one so that he could turn the original into coin.</p></aside>
    </section>

