    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">356 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander is born at Pella, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, to King Philip II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">336 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Philip II is assassinated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a> and Alexander is proclaimed king by the nobles and army at the age of 20</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">335 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After a campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> he razes rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and divides its territory among the other Boeotian cities</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">334 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">His army crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and defeats Persian forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus">Battle of the Granicus</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">333 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Issus</a>; Darius flees, leaving behind his wife, daughters and mother</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">331 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Victory over Darius at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a>, the final and decisive encounter between the two, is followed by the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">326 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He defeats King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-hydaspes">Battle of the Hydaspes</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and his army mutinies at the Hyphasis, refusing to march farther east</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">323 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, aged 32, while planning an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Lysippos, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alexander III was born at Pella, the capital of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, on a day that probably corresponds to 20 July 356 BCE, though the exact date is uncertain. His father was King Philip II of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a>; his mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoptolemus">Neoptolemus</a> I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, was Philip's fourth wife and for some time his principal one. Through Philip he could claim descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> and through his mother's family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>. The legends of his birth, among them the dreams that the biographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> reports and the story that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> was his father, may have emerged when Alexander was king, possibly at his own instigation.</p><p>He was raised in the manner of noble Macedonian youths, learning to read, play the lyre, ride, fight and hunt, under a nurse, Lanike, and the tutors Leonidas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a>. Plutarch tells how at ten he tamed a horse that had refused to be mounted; he named it Bucephalas, and it carried him as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. When Alexander was 13, Philip chose the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> as his tutor and gave the Temple of the Nymphs at Mieza as a classroom. There Alexander studied with the sons of Macedonian nobles such as Ptolemy, Hephaestion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>, many of them his future generals. Aristotle taught them medicine, philosophy, morals, religion, logic and art, and gave Alexander an annotated copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, which he later carried on his campaigns. Persian exiles living at Philip's court, among them Artabazos II, also gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a> a good knowledge of Persian affairs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alexander's education under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> ended at 16, when Philip, at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>, left him as regent and heir apparent. He put down a revolt of the Thracian Maedi and founded a city, Alexandropolis, in their territory. In 338 BCE father and son met the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> at Chaeronea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, where Philip commanded the right wing and Alexander the left; Alexander was the first to break the Theban lines. At Corinth Philip then formed the alliance of Greek states that modern scholars call the League of Corinth, was named its Hegemon and announced plans to attack the Persian Empire.</p><p>Philip's marriage in 338 BCE to Cleopatra Eurydice, niece of his general Attalus, made Alexander's position as heir less secure, since a son of hers would be fully Macedonian. After a quarrel at the wedding banquet Alexander fled in 337 BCE with his mother, returning after six months. In October 336 BCE Philip was assassinated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a> by Pausanias, the captain of his bodyguards, and the nobles and army proclaimed Alexander king on the spot, at the age of 20.</p><p>He began by eliminating potential rivals: his cousin Amyntas IV was executed, two princes of Lyncestis were killed, and he ordered the murder of Attalus. News of Philip's death roused Thebes, Athens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and the Thracian tribes to revolt. Alexander rode south with 3,000 cavalry, outflanked the Thessalians, and at Corinth took the title of Hegemon and the command against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. In 335 BCE he secured his northern frontier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>, and when Thebes rebelled again he razed the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus.jpg" alt="Battle of Issus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Battle of Issus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Issus_mosaic_-_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_-_Naples_2013-05-16_16-25-06_BW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 334 BCE Alexander's army crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> with approximately 48,100 soldiers, 6,100 cavalry and 120 ships. After a first victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus">Battle of the Granicus</a> he accepted the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, moved along the Ionian coast and took Halicarnassus by siege, seizing the coastal cities to deny the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> naval bases. In 333 BCE he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Issus</a>; Darius fled, leaving his family behind, and offered peace and a ransom of 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, which Alexander refused, replying that he was now king of Asia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> fell in 332 BCE after a long siege, and Gaza after three failed assaults.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was regarded as a liberator, Alexander sacrificed to the gods at Memphis, was pronounced son of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> by the oracle at the Siwa Oasis, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. In 331 BCE he marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and defeated Darius again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a>, their final and decisive encounter, then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, the treasury of Susa and the ceremonial capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>. Darius was stabbed on the orders of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessus">Bessus</a>, and Alexander buried him in a regal funeral, claiming to have been named his successor.</p><p>The pursuit of Bessus, who was executed in 329 BCE, became a campaign across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, where Alexander founded a series of cities called Alexandria and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a>, daughter of a Sogdian nobleman. He then invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> and in 326 BCE won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-hydaspes">Battle of the Hydaspes</a> against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a>, whom he made an ally and satrap. He was never defeated in battle, despite typically being outnumbered.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/persepolis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persepolis.jpg" alt="Persepolis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018-09-21_Iran,_Persepolis,_Tachara_(from_the_southeast).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Laurens R. Krol, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The conquests were violent. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and at Gaza the men of military age were killed and the women and children sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and the Roman historian Curtius writes that at Massaga Alexander slaughtered the entire population and reduced its buildings to rubble; a similar massacre followed at Ora. His troops looted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> for several days, and a fire that began in the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> spread through the city, by drunken accident or as deliberate revenge. Pre-Islamic Persian literature calls Alexander gujastak, "accursed", and accuses him of destroying temples and burning the sacred texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>.</p><p>Among his own men the gravest quarrels arose from his adoption of Persian dress and customs, notably proskynesis, a gesture the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> reserved for gods; he eventually abandoned it. After a plot was revealed, his officer Philotas was executed for failing to alert him, and Alexander ordered the assassination of Philotas's father, the general Parmenion. At Maracanda he personally killed Cleitus the Black, who had saved his life at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus">Granicus</a>, in a drunken altercation in which Cleitus accused him of forgetting Macedonian ways. His official historian Callisthenes, who had led the opposition to proskynesis, was implicated in a plot of the royal pages; it remains unclear whether he was involved.</p><p>Some Roman writers used Alexander as a cautionary tale of autocratic tendencies and anger. In his final years he showed signs of megalomania and paranoia, though his behaviour may also have been a practical attempt to hold a culturally disparate empire together.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/roxana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roxana.jpg" alt="Roxana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_The_Greate_and_Roxane_by_Rotari_1756.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pietro Rotari, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the Hyphasis River in 326 BCE the army, exhausted by years of campaigning and fearing the large armies farther east, mutinied and refused to go on. Alexander turned south along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>; while besieging the citadel of the Mallians he was nearly killed by an arrow that entered his lung. He lost many men in the Gedrosian Desert and reached Susa in 324 BCE. He executed several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> who had misbehaved in his absence, held a mass marriage of his senior officers to Persian and other noblewomen, and faced a second mutiny at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opis">Opis</a>, where his troops criticised the introduction of Persian officers and soldiers into Macedonian units. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a> his closest friend, Hephaestion, died of illness or poisoning, which devastated him.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, Alexander was planning an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> when he died in the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> on 10 or 11 June 323 BCE, at age 32. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> describes a fever that worsened after a night and day of drinking; Diodorus writes of pain after a large bowl of unmixed wine and no fever. Several ancient writers mention a theory of poisoning, which Plutarch dismissed as a fabrication; malaria and typhoid fever are among the natural causes suggested.</p><p>He left no obvious heir: his son Alexander IV was born to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a> after his death. According to Diodorus he bequeathed his kingdom "to the strongest"; other writers say he passed his signet ring to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a>. Alexander IV and Alexander's half-brother Philip III were made joint kings in name only, and both were later murdered. Forty years of war among the Successors followed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-romance.jpg" alt="Alexander Romance" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance">Alexander Romance</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_romance._Armenian_illuminated_manuscript_of_XIV_century_(Venice,_San_Lazzaro,_424).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At his death Alexander's empire covered some 5,200,000 square kilometres and was the largest state of its time. Out of the wars of his generals came three stable powers, Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Seleucid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and Antigonid Macedonia, and his death marks the conventional beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>. He founded more than twenty cities, the first and greatest being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in Egypt. A common Greek dialect, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">koine</a>, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of these lands, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> was written in it. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Greek culture mixed with Iranian and Buddhist cultures in what is known as Greco-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, while the power vacuum he left in the north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>. His constant demands for troops, however, depleted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> itself, which was later subjugated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> to the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> took him as a model. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance">Alexander Romance</a>, a text of legendary material, went through over one hundred recensions and translations across the European and Islamic worlds. Firdausi's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> places him in a line of legitimate Persian shahs, and most modern researchers believe the Qur'an's Dhu al-Qarnayn refers to him.</p><p>The accounts of those who knew Alexander are lost. The surviving histories, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-curtius-rufus">Quintus Curtius Rufus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and Justin, were written centuries later; Arrian, who used Ptolemy and Aristobulus, is generally considered the most reliable. Many scholars doubt the historicity of the "last plans" for further conquest that Diodorus ascribes to him.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Alexander the Great became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> in 336 BCE and in ten years of campaigning overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Persian Empire</a>, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> without losing a battle. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 323 BCE aged 32. His empire broke apart among his generals, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> and culture spread across the lands he had conquered.</em></p>

